<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">North Borneo</h1><p class="st-meta">1881 CE – 1946 CE · capital Sandakan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Territory in northern Borneo governed by a British chartered company, the last of its kind, until 1946.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1877 and 1878 the Austrian consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-von-overbeck">Baron von Overbeck</a> and the British merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dent">Alfred Dent</a> obtained grants of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate</a>. Dent bought out his partner, and in 1881 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> chartered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-north-borneo-company">British North Borneo Company</a> to govern the land. It became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1888.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The company ruled through a governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandakan">Sandakan</a> and a small staff, raising money from land concessions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, tobacco and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, and taxing trade. It built a railway and brought in Chinese labourers. Local resistance, notably the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mat-salleh">Mat Salleh</a> from 1894 to 1900, was suppressed, and indigenous peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadazan-dusun">Kadazan-Dusun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murut">Murut</a> faced new taxes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the territory from 1942; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandakan">Sandakan</a> death marches of 1945 killed thousands of Allied prisoners. Ruined by war, the company sold its rights to the British crown in 1946, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/crown-colony-of-north-borneo">Crown Colony of North Borneo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/north-borneo.jpg" alt="North Borneo" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Borneo_(1902%E2%80%931946).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1881 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sandakan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alfred Dent</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Sunni Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, English, Kadazan-Dusun</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British North Borneo, State of North Borneo, North Borneo Chartered Company</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1914&amp;focus=north-borneo" aria-label="North Borneo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7953 1959 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where North Borneo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8273 2215L8352 2207M8273 2215L4997 651M8273 2215L8065 2375M8273 2215L8216 2210"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8352 2207h0M4997 651h0M8065 2375h0M8216 2210h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8273 2215h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8273 2215h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sandakan. <a href="/atlas?year=1914&amp;focus=north-borneo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
